    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes.jpg" alt="Hernán Cortés" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Retrato_de_Hern%C3%A1n_Cort%C3%A9s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was born from the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> landed on the Gulf coast in 1519, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz-city">Veracruz</a>, and with the decisive help of indigenous allies, above all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlaxcalans">Tlaxcalans</a>, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, which fell on 13 August 1521 after a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic had already devastated its defenders. Cortés named the conquered lands New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> built on the ruins of the Aztec capital. Conquistadors spread out to subdue <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and the Gulf coast, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a>’s brutal campaigns in the 1530s created the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a> in the west. The Crown moved quickly to curb the power of the conquerors. It created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-mexico">Audiencia of Mexico</a>, a royal court and governing council, in 1527, and in 1535 appointed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, who governed until 1550 and laid the foundations of colonial administration, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> City mint and supporting the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, which began work in 1539. Franciscan, Dominican and Augustinian friars arrived from 1524 to convert the indigenous population. Over the following decades the viceroyalty’s authority came to extend over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, the Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, the northern frontier provinces and, after 1565, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zacatecas.jpg" alt="Zacatecas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Campo_en_zona_arqueol%C3%B3gica_La_Quemada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ErickBasM, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The conquest was followed by demographic catastrophe. Epidemics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a> and the diseases known as <em>cocoliztli</em>, especially in 1545–1548 and 1576–1580, together with war and forced labour, reduced the indigenous population of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> by perhaps 90 per cent by the early seventeenth century, although the size of the original population is disputed. Indigenous communities nevertheless survived as self-governing towns that paid tribute and supplied labour, first through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a></em> and later the <em>repartimiento</em>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">Silver</a> transformed the economy after the discoveries at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> in 1546 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> soon after; the patio process of amalgamation with mercury, developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-medina">Bartolomé de Medina</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachuca">Pachuca</a> in the 1550s, made mass production possible. Silver went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in the convoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz-city">Veracruz</a> and across the Pacific in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a>, where it bought Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>. Large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, wheat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> estates relied partly on enslaved Africans, perhaps 200,000 of whom were brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, mostly before 1650. A layered society emerged of Spaniards born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, American-born <em>criollos</em>, indigenous people, Africans and mixed groups classified as <em>castas</em>. The Catholic Church was the central institution of cultural life, and the devotion to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a>, said to have appeared to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-diego">Juan Diego</a> in 1531, became a shared focus of identity.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mixton-war.jpg" alt="Mixtón War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xochipilla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jaontiveros, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> kept only a small regular army, relying on conquistadors, militias and indigenous allies. Resistance did not end with the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixton-war">Mixtón War</a> of 1540–1542 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caxcanes">Caxcanes</a> and other peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-galicia">New Galicia</a> rose against Spanish rule, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> Mendoza himself led the army that crushed them with thousands of indigenous auxiliaries. The discovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> in the north provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chichimeca-war">Chichimeca War</a>, a long conflict along the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacatecas">Zacatecas</a> from about 1550 to 1590, which the Spaniards ended less by force than by a policy of gifts, settlement and missions. On the northern frontier, a line of presidios, or garrisoned forts, protected missions and mining towns against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apache">Apache</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comanche">Comanche</a> raids, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pueblo-revolt">Pueblo Revolt</a> of 1680 drove the Spaniards out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-vargas">Diego de Vargas</a> returned in 1692. At sea, the fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-juan-de-ulua">San Juan de Ulúa</a> guarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veracruz-city">Veracruz</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurens-de-graaf">Laurens de Graaf</a> sacked the port in 1683. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a> in 1762, the Crown built a regular army and an extensive colonial militia in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. These forces, commanded by royalist officers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/felix-maria-calleja">Félix María Calleja</a>, fought the insurgents during the war of independence from 1810, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-calderon-bridge">Bridge of Calderón</a> in January 1811.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez.jpg" alt="José de Gálvez" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez">José de Gálvez</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Jos%C3%A9_de_G%C3%A1lvez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> was the king’s personal representative, combining the roles of governor, captain-general and president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/real-audiencia-of-mexico">Audiencia of Mexico</a>. He answered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and at the end of his term his conduct was examined in a judicial review, the <em>residencia</em>. Other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">audiencias</a>, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> from 1543, Guadalajara from 1548 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from 1583, exercised judicial and administrative authority within the viceroyalty, and Guatemala, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> were ruled by captains-general who were largely autonomous in practice. Provinces were run by <em>corregidores</em> and <em>alcaldes mayores</em>. The law distinguished a republic of Spaniards from a republic of Indians, whose towns kept their own councils and nobles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mixtecs">Mixtec</a> and other languages were used in local records. The Crown held extensive rights over the Church under the royal patronage, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Inquisition</a> established a tribunal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> in 1571. Laws issued over two centuries were compiled in the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recopilacion-de-leyes-de-los-reinos-de-las-indias">Recopilación de Leyes de los Reinos de las Indias</a></em> of 1680. In the eighteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a>, driven by the inspector-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-galvez">José de Gálvez</a> from 1765, reorganised the colony: a royal tobacco monopoly, the expulsion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767, the Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces in 1776 and twelve intendancies in 1786 increased revenue and central control.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mexico-city.jpg" alt="Mexico City" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sobrevuelos_CDMX_HJ2A4913_(25514321687)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gobierno CDMX, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was the most valuable part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and a hinge of the first global economy. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, coined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> mint as the <em>real de a ocho</em> or Spanish dollar, circulated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and became a standard trade coin across the world, later serving as a model for the currencies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and China. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a>, sailing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acapulco">Acapulco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> from 1565 to 1815, connected the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and Asia directly for two and a half centuries. Crops, animals and techniques were exchanged across the Atlantic and Pacific, carrying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a> and chillies outward and introducing wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, horses and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Culturally, New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> produced a distinctive baroque civilisation: the cathedrals of Mexico City and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puebla-state">Puebla</a>, the poet and scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sor-juana-ines-de-la-cruz">Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz</a>, the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-de-siguenza-y-gongora">Carlos de Sigüenza y Góngora</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-and-pontifical-university-of-mexico">Royal University of Mexico</a>, founded in 1551. Indigenous and Spanish traditions mixed in religion, art and language, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a> became a lasting symbol. The viceroyalty shaped the borders, legal systems and cities of modern Mexico and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, and left Spanish place names, missions and law across the south-western United States, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, as well as a lasting Hispanic and Catholic imprint on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt.jpg" alt="Alexander von Humboldt" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stieler,_Joseph_Karl_-_Alexander_von_Humboldt_-_1843.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joseph Karl Stieler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> reached its peak of wealth around 1800, when it had some six million inhabitants, its mines were producing more <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> than ever, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-von-humboldt">Alexander von Humboldt</a> described <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> as one of the finest cities built by Europeans. Yet resentment was growing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> favoured Spanish-born officials over criollos, and the Consolidation of 1804 forced the Church to call in loans, damaging landowners and merchants. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Spanish Bourbons</a> to abdicate in 1808, Spanish merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> City overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-iturrigaray">José de Iturrigaray</a> to prevent criollo moves towards self-government. On 16 September 1810 the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-hidalgo-y-costilla">Miguel Hidalgo</a> launched a mass revolt at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dolores-hidalgo">Dolores</a>, which took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a> but was defeated in 1811; Hidalgo was executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-morelos">José María Morelos</a> continued the struggle, convening the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-chilpancingo">Congress of Chilpancingo</a> in 1813, until his capture and execution in 1815. Royalist armies then contained the insurgency, which survived as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> warfare under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vicente-guerrero">Vicente Guerrero</a>. The end came from within the royalist camp. After liberal revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1820, the royalist officer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a> agreed with Guerrero on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plan-of-iguala">Plan of Iguala</a> of February 1821, promising independence, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and equality. On 24 August 1821 the newly arrived Juan O’Donojú signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a>, and in September Iturbide’s army entered Mexico City, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> was the richest and most populous viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, governing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a> for three centuries; its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and Pacific <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> helped to create the first global economy, and its society laid the foundations of modern Mexico.</em></p>

